n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi {r}^{2})+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2})+4(2) \\\\  & =2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,3).$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)(3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi +8)+6 \\\\  & =2\\pi +14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-49209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8ddb95531a97f38217f742fcfc2b96f34ca54152b25c7ff23e8ac70cfc220ca1"} {"question" "q-49209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-49209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi +10"] ["math" "2\\pi +14"] ["math" "4\\pi +12"] ["math" "\\pi +11"] ["math" "\\pi +16"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-49209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi +14"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-14785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/824f039d5f015a2cd2165cfd469c44e7f3e5a0cda6416964ec91d57de4ca06eb.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-14785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1),$ the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle of radius of $1,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-14785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8ddb95531a97f38217f742fcfc2b96f34ca54152b25c7ff23e8ac70cfc220ca1"} {"question" "q-14785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-14785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{11}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{11}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\pi +\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\pi -\\frac{11}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-14785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{11}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-339264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/db123092734fe2d84dacd2da2d005d001aadb673d1ad7f30c9527ef9f5a8ba7c.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-339264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-1)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-1,3].$ Using the additive property of integrals, we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-1).$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,3].$ The corresponding region is a semicircle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+2\\pi  \\\\  & =8+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-339264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8ddb95531a97f38217f742fcfc2b96f34ca54152b25c7ff23e8ac70cfc220ca1"} {"question" "q-339264" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-339264" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8+2\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-86178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/8f9b82ba9e1ed640aedd6f67118beef2d88c419bbd046171cca5b527c