estion-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-117985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-1],$ the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-1].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,3].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle of radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =8-2\\pi -2 \\\\  & =6-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-117985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8ddb95531a97f38217f742fcfc2b96f34ca54152b25c7ff23e8ac70cfc220ca1"} {"question" "q-117985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-117985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\pi "] ["math" "3-2\\pi "] ["math" "3-\\pi "] ["math" "4\\pi "] ["math" "6-2\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "q-117985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6-2\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "568859e73f70e8f5f3b8a5a2d706777d731a1e188d3d85b8268271113f30e91c"} {"question" "e-432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/example-432.json" "file_sha256" "56e94a6e43f7791b16ce88d775bd5d24166385feb3298658bf6fba5ab6cec906"} {"question" "e-432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x{|}_{-2}^{0}+({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =[3(0)-3(-2)]+[({5}^{2}+3(5))-({0}^{2}+3(0))] \\\\  & =6+25+15 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/example-432.json" "file_sha256" "56e94a6e43f7791b16ce88d775bd5d24166385feb3298658bf6fba5ab6cec906"} {"question" "e-433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/ad45cbdcd126df931f1a35c60ca12b45d520a2a0a5cddf4fa012c0515d3024c7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/example-433.json" "file_sha256" "92da86a3ba3155597b55c6fa9c94e1533ad1e5a117d41cc23532b4a74747de55"} {"question" "e-433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x \\in [0,2)$ and the line segment for $x \\in [2,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{-A_2}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x =-A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/example-433.json" "file_sha256" "92da86a3ba3155597b55c6fa9c94e1533ad1e5a117d41cc23532b4a74747de55"} {"question" "e-434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/36c6a1c84e0b0c32a5fd35fd422645f60340da6d205dd8cc31054cd558c2f5f9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/example-434.json" "file_sha256" "519c2e5e20e1150e255fe2990fc53f0ca2bc38daa83cf9a835773db656b47db7"} {"question" "e-434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ a line segment for $x\\in (0,2),$ a line segment for $x\\in (2,4],$ and the point at $x=2.$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+0+{{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (2,4].$  The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi -2+2 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/example-434.json" "file_sha256" "519c2e5e20e1150e255fe2990fc53f0ca2bc38daa83cf9a835773db656b47db7"}] "usage" {"e-433" [] "e-432" [] "e-434" [] "q-14785" [] "q-5046" [] "q-49212" []} "basis" 1150 "retractions" [[17592186078972 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of\n$∫_{-3}^{4}f(x)dx$?\n$\\displaystyle f(x)=\\begin{cases}4, & x<0, \\\\ -x+4, & x\\ge 0.\\end{cases}$"] [17592186078949 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/626/Source/Images/q-49212.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$, shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $∫_{-3}^{3}f(x)dx$."] [17592186078661 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/626/Source/Images/q-14785.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y = f(x)$ above, find the value of\n$∫_{-3}^{4}f(x)dx$."] [17592186078230 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,$?\n${3,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 2x + 3,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0$"] [17592186078230 :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-2}^{5}f(x)dx &= ∫_{-2}^{0}3dx + ∫_{0}^{5}(2x + 3)dx \\\\\n&= 3x \\mid _{-2}^{0} + (x^{2} + 3x) \\mid _{0}^{5} \\\\\n&= [3(0) - 3(-2)] + [(5^{2} + 3(5)) - (0^{2} + 3(0))] \\\\\n&= 6 + 25 + 15 \\\\\n&= 46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186078231 :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$, shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/626/Source/Images/e969825468755643c99584897eed6f04.png)"] [17592186078231 :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $[MATH: x \\in [0,2)]$ and the line segment for $x \\in [2,4]$. Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{-A_2}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $[MATH: x \\in [0,2).]$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{1}|= bh; = 2 \\cdot 2; = 4$.\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4]$. The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2}bh; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2; = 2$.\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n$\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x =-A_2 = -2$.\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{4}f(x)dx &= A_{1} - A_{2} \\\\\n&= 4 - 2 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186078232 :question/problem "Given the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/626/Source/Images/eb1ab74ae544a1b33f0f6c89141309e6.png)"] [17592186078232 :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0]$, a line segment for $x\\in (0,2)$, a line segment for $[MATH: x\\in (2,4],]$ and the point at $x=2$. So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-4}^{4}f(x)dx &= ∫_{-4}^{0}f(x)dx + ∫_{0}^{2}f(x)dx + ∫_{2}^{2}f(x)dx + ∫_{2}^{4}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-4}^{0}f(x)dx_(⏟)_(A_{1}) + ∫_{0}^{2}f(x)dx_(⏟)_(-A_{2}) + 0 + ∫_{2}^{4}f(x)dx_(⏟)_(A_{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0]$. The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2$, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2}πr^{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot π \\cdot 2^{2}; = 2π$.\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2)$. The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2}bh; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2; = 2$.\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n$\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2$.\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $[MATH: x \\in (2,4].]$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2}bh; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2; = 2$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-4}^{4}f(x)dx &= A_{1} - A_{2} + A_{3} \\\\\n&= 2π - 2 + 2 \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186075276 17592186075277 17592186075278 17592186075279 17592186075280 17592186076906 17592186076907 17592186076908 17592186076909 17592186076910 17592186077044 17592186077045 17592186077046 17592186077047 17592186077048] "immutable_fields" [[17592186075275 :answer-field/correct] [17592186075275 :answer-field/id] [17592186075275 :answer-field/key] [17592186075275 :answer-field/type] [17592186076905 :answer-field/correct] [17592186076905 :answer-field/id] [17592186076905 :answer-field/key] [17592186076905 :answer-field/type] [17592186077043 :answer-field/correct] [17592186077043 :answer-field/id] [17592186077043 :answer-field/key] [17592186077043 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
